\details{
\subsection{Distribution of time in the section}{

An age-depth model distributes time over the stratigraphic section. The amount of time contained in a stratigraphic increment is given by the condensation \eqn{c(h)} (time per stratigraphic increment, see \code{\link[=condensation]{condensation()}}), the inverse of the sedimentation rate. Hiatuses represent time that is not preserved in the section: they have zero thickness and contribute no time to the distribution. The total preserved time is \eqn{\int c(h) \, dh}, i.e. the duration of the age-depth model minus the duration of its hiatuses.

\code{diss_KL} compares the distributions of time \eqn{\lambda_x(h) = \mathrm{rate} \cdot c_x(h)} and \eqn{\lambda_y(h) = \mathrm{rate} \cdot c_y(h)} of \code{x} and \code{y}. The factor \code{rate} converts time into an expected number of events, and gives the KL divergence its probabilistic interpretation: If events (e.g. fossils, samples, or geochemical signals) occur in time as a homogeneous Poisson point process with rate \code{rate}, an age-depth model places them in the section as an inhomogeneous Poisson point process with intensity \eqn{\lambda(h)}. Events that occur during a hiatus are not preserved. The KL divergence then measures how well the stratigraphic record of events expected under \code{y} describes the record expected under \code{x}.
}

\subsection{Decomposition}{

The KL divergence of the distribution of time of \code{x} from the one of \code{y} is
\deqn{\mathrm{KL} = \int \lambda_x \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y) - \lambda_x + \lambda_y \, dh = \mathrm{volume} + \mathrm{position}.}
Let \eqn{\Lambda = \int \lambda \, dh} be the preserved time multiplied by \code{rate} (the expected number of preserved events), and \eqn{p = \lambda / \Lambda} the distribution of the preserved time within the section, normalized to one. The volume component
\deqn{\mathrm{volume} = \Lambda_x \log(\Lambda_x / \Lambda_y) - \Lambda_x + \Lambda_y}
only depends on how much time the two age-depth models preserve. It is zero if both preserve the same amount of time, irrespective of where in the section. It equals the KL divergence between the Poisson distributed numbers of preserved events. The position component
\deqn{\mathrm{position} = \Lambda_x \, \mathrm{shape}, \quad \mathrm{shape} = \mathrm{KL}(p_x \| p_y)}
only depends on where in the section the time is preserved. It is zero if both age-depth models distribute their preserved time in the same proportions over the section, irrespective of how much time they preserve. The shape term compares the normalized distributions of preserved time. It is independent of \code{rate} and of the time unit, whereas \code{kl}, \code{volume}, \code{position}, and \eqn{\Lambda} scale linearly with \code{rate}. In terms of events, the shape term is the position divergence per preserved event.
}

\subsection{Symmetrized divergence}{

The KL divergence is not symmetric. \code{kl_reverse} is the KL divergence of the distribution of time of \code{y} from the one of \code{x}, and \code{kl_sym} is the symmetrized (Jeffreys) divergence
\deqn{\mathrm{kl\_sym} = \mathrm{KL}(x \| y) + \mathrm{KL}(y \| x) = \int (\lambda_x - \lambda_y) \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y) \, dh,}
which is symmetric in \code{x} and \code{y}. The components of the reverse direction are stored in the element \code{reverse}.

All calculations are exact: The condensation of both age-depth models is constant between consecutive tie points of the two age-depth models, so all integrals reduce to finite sums.
}

\subsection{Sections without time and the convention 0 log 0 = 0}{

Age-depth models have strictly increasing time tie points (see \code{\link[=is_adm]{is_adm()}}), so the condensation is strictly positive everywhere within the stratigraphic range of an age-depth model: every stratigraphic increment contains some time. Stratigraphic intervals without time therefore only occur if \code{h_range} extends beyond the range of \code{x} or \code{y}. Outside of its range, an age-depth model has no sediment and contains no time. The following cases can occur:
\itemize{
\item Within the range of both models (in particular for the default \code{h_range}), both models assign time everywhere and all divergences are finite.
\item Where \eqn{\lambda_x = 0 < \lambda_y}, the term \eqn{\lambda_x \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y)} is 0 (convention \eqn{0 \log 0 = 0}), and the interval contributes \eqn{\int \lambda_y \, dh} to the KL divergence: \code{y} places time where \code{x} places none, which is penalized only by the amount of that time.
\item Where \eqn{\lambda_x > 0 = \lambda_y}, \code{x} places time where \code{y} places none (in terms of events: \code{x} predicts events that are impossible under \code{y}), and the KL divergence, its position component and the shape term are \code{Inf}. If in addition \eqn{\Lambda_y = 0}, the volume component is \code{Inf} as well.
\item If \eqn{\Lambda_x = 0} (\code{h_range} does not overlap with \code{x}), \code{x} preserves no time in \code{h_range}, the position component is 0 by convention, the shape term is undefined (\code{NA}), and the KL divergence equals the volume component \eqn{\Lambda_y}.
\item If neither model preserves time in \code{h_range}, all divergences are 0, and the shape terms are \code{NA}.
\item \code{kl_sym} is \code{Inf} if either direction is \code{Inf}, i.e. whenever exactly one of the models places time on part of \code{h_range}.
}
}
}
